Z||000000||VISCERAL||07-12-99||01:23 PM||||Hi, finished fixing my car:<br>fresh coolant<br>redline waterwetter<br>160' thermostat<br>176' turnon fan switch-off at 160'<br>hypertech chip<br>exhaust wrap- y pipe to cat- really helps<br>MY PROBLEM: cold idle fine but once warmed still comes down too slow 1200-850 at intersections. runs fine except for that. <br>running  too rich????, i think so. <br>should I:<br>put back the stock chip?<br>put in 180' stat?<br>i wanna keep the switch in, so......help<br>maybe clear the computer and take it out on the highway for a steady 20 minutes to clean out the bugs and let the computer readjust?<br>help!!!!!!!!
Z||000001||Eric||07-12-99||05:32 PM||||Did you say your idle was too slow or too slow to come down?  My car idles between 800 and 1200 rpm.  That doesn't seem too low to me.<p>Did you use exaust wrap on your manifolds too?  I wanted to try this to keep the temp down, but if it causes problems???
Z||000002||VISCERAL||07-12-99||10:49 PM||||now as I come to a stop with my 5 speed the idle will take its time to come down 1500 rpm or it will go up to about 1500 if I kept it in gear and let it drop to 800 before letting the car out of gear. The temp gauge never goes over 160 and likes to hover around 140' in 80' heat. I'll try to pull a code out of it tomorrow.<p>the thermowrap's instructions says if it was a carbed car that the car would richen up and <br>as a suggestion lean out your jets. good stuff-don't forget about spraying the wrap afterwards with its protective spray<p>i think i just went overkill?
Z||000003||Carrolles||07-13-99||07:12 AM||||140 degrees may be a bit too low for normal operation.  The ECM might interpert this as a cold engine.
Z||000004||DJRice||07-13-99||08:38 AM||||Sounds to me like the ECM is trying to warm the engine up.  You probably should go back up to the normal temp thermostat.  The temp will affect how the gas burns, If its not hot enough the fuel wont burn like it should,  Especially if you are running high octane gas.  The higher the octane, the hotter the temp will need to be to burn the fuel properly.  <p>I suspect that if you change the thermostat back to the higher temp, you will fix the problem.
